WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.240
Bob Newby, also known as “Bob the Brain”&nbsp;
or “Bob Newby: Super Hero,” was born in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.800
the early 1940s in Maine. He spent much of his&nbsp;
early childhood there, until he was a pre-teen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.400
when he moved to Hawkins, Indiana. He&nbsp;
attended Hawkins Middle School and later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.080
Hawkins High School where he developed a&nbsp;
crush on a classmate named Joyce Byers. Bob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.720
was likely a social outcast with a keen ability&nbsp;
for solving puzzles and understanding technology.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.360
He had a passion for tech as&nbsp;
early as his middle school years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.640
and started a petition and fundraiser&nbsp;
to start the AV Club at his school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.440
an organization that would last&nbsp;
well beyond his tenure there.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.640
It’s because of these skills, that when&nbsp;
Radioshack first opened in Hawkins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.680
Bob became an employee, climbing&nbsp;
the ranks to store manager.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.520
Being originally from Maine, being a social&nbsp;
outcast and being able to see patterns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.440
solve puzzles that others cannot, gives Bob&nbsp;
many common traits found in characters from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.680
the Stephen King universe. Bob might even&nbsp;
have a little bit of “the shine”, the psychic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.360
and clairvoyant ability most prominently&nbsp;
featured in Stephen King’s The Shining.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.920
To find out how Bob could&nbsp;
be a Stephen King character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.840
stick around until the end of this video.&nbsp;
This video is sponsored by Audible.

00:00:58.754 --> 00:01:02.086
(chaotic piano)

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.640
Bob wouldn’t be the first time Stranger Things&nbsp;
would exude some inspiration from Stephen King.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.840
There seem to be comparisons between Eleven and&nbsp;
various King characters, most specifically Carrie.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.000
– Says she’s special. Born with “abilities”.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.400
– Abilities?

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.920
– You read any Stephen King?

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.920
– [Zac] The Stranger Things creators, The Duffer Brothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.760
have made it very clear that Stephen King is&nbsp;
a direct inspiration for much of what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.320
do. In an interview at the Emmy Awards in 2017,&nbsp;
after the release of Stranger Things season one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.840
they share what an impact Stephen&nbsp;
King has had on them and the show.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.120
– Stephen King… just, I haven’t met him in person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.160
but he tweeted about our show and we’ve&nbsp;
exchanged some emails with him and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.640
still blows my mind, because he… growing&nbsp;
up, he was such an inspiration. I mean he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.560
like a God to us and so… it’s been surreal&nbsp;
communicating with him at all. It’s been amazing.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.600
– But while Stephen King themes are&nbsp;
apparent throughout Stranger Things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.520
Bob Newby may in fact, be the biggest&nbsp;
Stephen King nod of them all. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.840
video I’ll be analysing his entire story,&nbsp;
so let’s take it back to his introduction.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.400
(synth music)

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.480
In October 1984, just before Halloween,&nbsp;
Bob appears in the general store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.880
as he asks Joyce Byers if they have jack o’lantern&nbsp;
buckets in any other colors, besides orange. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.640
does this to get Joyce alone so they can make&nbsp;
out in the stockroom, like teenagers. This,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.160
objectively bad, pick up line and their&nbsp;
familiarity with each other makes it clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.480
that they have been in a romantic relationship&nbsp;
for a little while. As they “talk” in the storeroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.640
Bob expresses that he feels like a teenager&nbsp;
again, but that when he was an actual teenager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.160
Joyce had no idea that Bob even existed,&nbsp;
despite them going to the same school.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.720
This indicates that Joyce was&nbsp;
more popular in high school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.120
and it’s hinted that Bob might have been bullied.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.320
– [Hopper] How’s uh, Bob the Brain.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.960
– [Joyce] Don’t call him that.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.400
– Sorry. Old Habit

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.960
– The stereotypes carry over into their adulthood.&nbsp;
Hopper is the sheriff and Bob is the store manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.280
for Radioshack, they’re effectively the jock and&nbsp;
nerd of the real world. High school never ends.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.560
This would make Bob very similar to&nbsp;
the members of The Losers Club in IT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.760
as they are also outcasts who&nbsp;
are targeted by Henry Bowers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.400
and similar to Carrie from the novel… Carrie, in&nbsp;
that she’s a complete outcast who is tormented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.320
by the other students. Or Arnie Cunningham&nbsp;
from Christine, the list goes on and on.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.720
Bob’s excitement about being with&nbsp;
his dream girl from high school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.680
makes it seem like he would have been&nbsp;
very awkward around girls as a teenager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.167
I’m reminded of how the boys&nbsp;
are with Beverly Marsh from IT.

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:26.505
(hip hop music)

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.080
But being an outcast is only the first trait&nbsp;
that makes Bob very Stephen King-esque.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.120
He starts to suggest to Joyce that she&nbsp;
should move her family out of Hawkins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.400
mentioning that his parents are thinking of&nbsp;
selling their house in Maine. The fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.640
Bob’s family has a house in Maine means Bob&nbsp;
probably spent some of his childhood there.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.840
Many of Stephen King’s stories take&nbsp;
place in different parts of Maine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.960
from IT taking place in the fictional&nbsp;
town of Derry, to The Deadzone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.360
set in another fictional town of Castle Rock,&nbsp;
along with many other Stephen King stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.080
So Bob probably grew up in an environment very&nbsp;
similar to a lot of Stephen King’s characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.400
Unfortunately Joyce rejects this idea, so his&nbsp;
parent’s home in Maine is never mentioned again.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.840
One morning, Bob gives Joyce’s son&nbsp;
Will a ride to school. By this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.040
Bob knows that Will has gone through&nbsp;
something bad but he doesn’t know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.960
exactly what. He suspects that Will is having&nbsp;
nightmares, or something along those lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.800
and offers guidance by telling a story. He&nbsp;
describes meeting a clown at a fair when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:22.000
he was a kid. The clown’s name is “Mr. Baldo.”&nbsp;
“Baldo” happens to sound similar to “Bozo” who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.920
as I mentioned in my Horror History episode on&nbsp;
Adrian Melon from IT, was one of the influences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.080
that Stephen King attributes to creating&nbsp;
one of his most iconic monsters: Pennywise.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.280
Even the voice that Bob imitates&nbsp;
when describing Mr. Baldo sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.080
very similar to Pennywise&nbsp;
from IT and IT: Chapter Two.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.288
– [Bob] Hey Kiddo, would you like a balloon?

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.160
– I bet I could cheer him up!&nbsp;
I’ll give him a balloon!

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.040
But Bob’s clown imitation is not&nbsp;
the only detail that would make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.120
it seem like Bob may have encountered&nbsp;
a creature very similar to Pennywise.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.440
As a Stranger Things fan, you may have assumed&nbsp;
after watching the series, reading the comics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.280
