00:05:56.415 --> 00:06:00.351
(upbeat music)

00:06:00.351 --> 00:06:04.240
Bob goes on to explain to Will that he couldn’t&nbsp;
seem to get the clown Baldo “out of his head”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.320
and that “every night Mr. Baldo would come&nbsp;
to him in his dreams.” This is very similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.840
to how Pennywise would work his way into&nbsp;
the dreams and hallucinations of the seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:15.760
members of The Loser’s Club. One that comes&nbsp;
to mind is Ben Hanscom’s baseball dream:

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.760
“He had finally fallen asleep and dreamed he was&nbsp;
playing baseball with the other boys in the vacant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:24.240
lot behind Tracker Brothers’ Truck Depot. He had&nbsp;
just hit a bases-clearing home run, … In the dream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.000
he was almost bursting with pride and happiness&nbsp;
. . . and then he had looked out toward center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.160
field, where a chainlink fence marked the boundary&nbsp;
between the cindery lot and the weedy ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.560
beyond that sloped into the Barrens. A figure was&nbsp;
standing in those tangled weeds and low bushes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.480
almost out of sight... It was a clown...&nbsp;
When Ben awoke the next morning he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:44.880
forgotten the dream but his pillow was damp to&nbsp;
the touch . . . as if he had wept in the night."

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:48.000
Bob shares a lot of similarities&nbsp;
with Ben Hanscom, specifically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.320
Ben struggles with weight and Bob appears to&nbsp;
as well. Perhaps because of this, both choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.480
to see themselves as having heroic qualities&nbsp;
when squaring up against enemies. Ben chooses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.360
to see himself as an athlete and Bob repeatedly&nbsp;
refers to himself as “Bob Newby: Superhero.”

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.960
Bob goes on to describe that Baldo kept&nbsp;
coming to him for months, until one day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.880
the nightmares suddenly stopped. Pennywise&nbsp;
only comes out for a few months at a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.680
every twenty seven years, so&nbsp;
it would make sense that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.000
COULD be Pennywise or at the very least, a&nbsp;
heavy Stranger Things homage to Pennywise.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.600
There are also some specific details from&nbsp;
the Stranger Things Comic Book series that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.640
may also pay tribute to Stephen&nbsp;
King’s popular painted antagonist.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:26.080
Will tells a story that occurred in&nbsp;
the 1950s about a tentacled monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.000
referred to as “The Child Eater of Hawkins”&nbsp;
that shows up roughly every twenty years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.400
and makes kids disappear in sets of exactly eight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.400
before disappearing again. The monster’s pattern&nbsp;
is nearly identical to that of Pennywise.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.920
Also, the movies on the Hawkins movie theater&nbsp;
marquee are showing The Blob and The Mummy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:43.440
The Mummy was one of the forms that Pennywise&nbsp;
took to scare the members of The Losers Club,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.200
and The Blob would certainly fit in with that too

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:48.160
The child eater of Hawkins is revealed&nbsp;
to have been made up, however, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.240
doesn’t mean that it couldn’t be based on urban&nbsp;
legends passed down front the older generation.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.960
But back to the conversation with&nbsp;
Bob and Will in the car. Bob goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.480
on to explain that he truly defeated&nbsp;
Baldo by refusing to run away anymore.

00:08:00.622 --> 00:08:02.622
– [Bob] Well I'd fall asleep...

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.156
...and just like always, Mr. Baldo came to&nbsp;
me. Only, I didn’t run. I stood my ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.156 --> 00:08:09.920
I looked at Baldo, in his stupid face, and I&nbsp;
said, “Go away. GO AWAY!” and just like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:14.000
he was gone, never saw him&nbsp;
again. 

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.600
I know It: Chapter 2 isn’t Stephen&nbsp;
King canon, but I can’t help but think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.200
about how the Losers Club takes their stand&nbsp;
against Pennywise by refusing to be afraid.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.480
Danny Torance in The Shining, uses&nbsp;
this same tactic to protect himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.040
from some of the horrors of the&nbsp;
Overlook Hotel throughout the book.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.280
In the follow up, Doctor Sleep, the ghost of&nbsp;
Dick Hallorann explains exactly why this specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.360
technique of standing your ground and refusing to&nbsp;
be afraid can work for those who have “The Shine”.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:44.240
– World’s a hungry place, and the darkest things&nbsp;
are the hungriest and they’ll eat what shines,&nbsp;&nbsp;swarm it

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:48.389
like mosquitos or leaches.&nbsp;
Can’t do nothin’ about that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:56.560
What you can do is turn what&nbsp;
they’ve come for against them.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.680
– This explains why Bob standing&nbsp;
his ground and yelling “go away”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.720
repeatedly to Mr. Baldo would have&nbsp;
worked, if he had some shine to him.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.330
Unfortunately this stand your&nbsp;
ground tactic, does not work out for&nbsp;&nbsp;Will.

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:09.760
Bob once again exhibits a potential shine&nbsp;
later when he drops by Joyce’s house, under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.320
the impression that Will is “sick” and offers Will&nbsp;
some mind teasers. It’s here that Joyce suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:18.960
realizes that Bob has a unique skill for solving&nbsp;
puzzles and being able to see what others can’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.600
when it comes to finding solutions.
She asks him to help her figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.600
what Will’s drawings mean… of course not sharing&nbsp;
with him that Will is partially possessed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.040
Mind Flayer and that these drawings are coming&nbsp;
from that part of Will’s brain, but Bob is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:33.600
nice guy who goes along with it anyway. Sure&nbsp;
enough, Bob is able to see what others can’t.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.320
– [Joyce] What? What is it?

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.760
– [Bob] I know that shape. It’s Lover’s&nbsp;
Lake.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.400
– [Zac] Even though Joyce still doesn’t&nbsp;
fill Bob in on what’s going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.480
he goes with them to the underground tunnels&nbsp;
where Jim Hopper is trapped and Bob participates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:49.840
in saving him… despite the implication that&nbsp;
they never really got along in high school.

00:09:50.812 --> 00:09:53.280
– Hi, Bob.
– Hey Jim.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.360
– [Zac] Bob is rushed into the Hawkins Lab with Joyce,&nbsp;
Will, Mike Wheeler and Hopper, where Joyce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.326
FINALLY fills Bob in on what’s going on,&nbsp;
leading Bob to coin his own new nickname.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.720
– I always thought stuff like this only&nbsp;
happened in movies and comic books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.040
certainly not in Hawkins and&nbsp;
certainly not to someone like you.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.320
– Or you.
– Yeah. Bob Newby: Superhero.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.080
– [Zac] And that nickname is quickly put to the test,&nbsp;
as shortly after this, as the creatures known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:23.840
as the demodogs escape from the Upside&nbsp;
Down and take over Hawkins lab. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:28.320
able to knock out the power, stranding Bob with&nbsp;
Hopper; Mike; Joyce; Will’s doctor, Sam Owens;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:31.920
and an unconscious Will, locked away in&nbsp;
a safe room on an upper level of the lab.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.800
As Hopper risks his life to get to the&nbsp;
basement to turn the breakers back on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.040
Bob intervenes with his expert “nerd” knowledge&nbsp;
of computer systems. He explains to Hopper that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:42.880
turning on the breakers may restore the power,&nbsp;
but it would take an entire reboot of the system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:46.880
to open the gates, so that they can escape.&nbsp;
It becomes immediately clear that the computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:50.720
programming language “basic” is required to&nbsp;
do this and Bob is the only one who knows it.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:53.840
To further the contrast of Bob’s&nbsp;
“nerdiness” against Hopper being Sheriff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:56.880
Bob has no idea how to use a gun,&nbsp;
requiring Hopper to teach him.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:00.320
Bob does make it down safely and is able&nbsp;
to re-start the breakers and employ his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:03.600
knowledge of Basic to get everything up and&nbsp;
running and open the gates. But just when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.280
it seems like he’s in the clear, the demodogs&nbsp;
appear on a stairwell on the computer screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:11.280
putting both him and everyone else in danger,&nbsp;
but once again, Bob puts his understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:15.040
of Basic to use by putting in the code to&nbsp;
start the fire sprinklers in the stairwell.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:17.840
Joyce, Hopper and the kids make&nbsp;
a break for the exit while Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.880
Owens stays behind to guide Bob over&nbsp;
the intercom to safety. This whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:24.640
ordeal is very tense. Bob brushes&nbsp;
shoulders with death several times.

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:28.720
Sadly for Bob, just as it seems like he’s in&nbsp;
the clear with the demodogs trapped locked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:31.680
out on the other side of the door like&nbsp;
The Bumpus’s dogs on Christmas morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:34.960
one of them breaks through, like the&nbsp;
Bumpus’s dogs on Christmas morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:40.117
and Bob ends up on the wrong end of one of the&nbsp;
most tragic deaths that Hawkins has had to endure.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.080
BUT Bob’s memory perseveres. It is, surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:54.160
Mike Wheeler, who mourns Bob after he’s gone&nbsp;
and points out that it was actually Bob who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:58.801
originally started the A.V. Club that the kids&nbsp;
have been so active in, for the past two years.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:03.200
– He petitioned the school to start&nbsp;
it and everything. Then he had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:10.966
fundraiser for equipment. Mr. Clarke learned&nbsp;
everything from him. Pretty awesome, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.966 --> 00:12:12.412
– [Dustin] Yeah.
– [Lucas] Yeah...

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:16.480
– We can’t let him die in vain.

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:20.320
– Bob would become an unlikely hero for the&nbsp;
newest generation of Hawkins Middle School&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:25.040
nerds and truly lived up to his reputation&nbsp;
as a classic Stephen King-esque outcast.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:29.920
If you want to see the histories of more&nbsp;
Stranger Bois, click that playlist on the left.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:34.720
I’ve got more episodes on the way, so remember to&nbsp;
subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:41.354
ring the deathbell for notifications and I’ll see&nbsp;
you in the next one. Assuming we both survive.

00:12:41.354 --> 00:12:45.469
(industrial dance music)

